const theme=`A theme is a versioned visual production spec, not a palette dump, adjective prompt or geometry generator. It stores visual language, reusable color/ramp/material/outline/light tokens, hard and advisory rules, limited size/class adaptations, reference metadata and source evidence. It never generates geometry, paints pixels, chooses parts or replaces modeling. One scene binds at most one theme; there is no subtree or multi-theme engine. The library tool is zpixel_theme with add/read/list/revise; binding is the outer theme parameter of zpixel_scene; checks reuse zpixel_inspect kind:'theme' (returns {document,revision,theme_report}), preview and export. The renderer remains 0.3.6; the package and service are 0.4.0 for the theme contract, and the current 0.5.3-dev release adds model-first observation without changing theme semantics.

Canonical revision. add assigns format dsh-zpixel-theme/1, the id and revision 0. The author spec omits format/id/revision; title and summary are required and every other section defaults empty. The canonical Theme keeps tags (bounded strings), signature (existing rule ids, never a second copy of rule text), language (identity/view/scale/form/pixel/color/outline/light/materials/detail; each an authored string, omitted means unspecified), tokens, rules, adaptations, references and source. Rule scope minSize/maxSize refer to max(scene.width,scene.height). adaptations may change only language sections for a size band or asset class; they never override tokens or hard rules. source records kind concept/reference/hybrid plus brief, observations, inferences, decisions and unknowns. The hash is bare SHA-256 hex64 of stable sorted-key canonical JSON, never an author field. Revisions are immutable; revise takes the observed expected_revision and a complete replacement spec, not a patch. The same txid retry returns its fixed applied revision; a changed payload under the same txid errors. add/revise return compact theme identity {id,revision,title} plus hash, capabilities, replayed and appliedRevision; they do not resend the full spec. read defaults to the head; pass revision to pin.

Authoring route and evidence. Reference-based: inspect real images, record what is visible as observations, keep interpretations as inferences, extract reusable roles and construction rules rather than an object inventory, and mark unknowns. A JPEG or resized sample cannot claim the original palette, and one image cannot prove a cross-asset law. Concept-based: start from a written brief, turn adjectives into form/color/outline/pixel/light decisions, state allowed variation and prohibitions, and label the source kind honestly; hybrid is allowed. The service never calls another model, never analyzes an image in the background and never copies reference pixels. A text-only route can store analysis authored elsewhere, but the tool result must not claim the service inspected the image; route capability is reported separately as image_capable, data_only or capability_unknown. No reference count, confidence score or sample count promotes a rule to must; the user or an explicit design decision does. references are metadata only: workspace-relative image path (delivery supports PNG/JPEG/WebP/GIF), optional bare hex64 sha256, role reference/positive/negative, note, crop, observations and inferences. read returns availability metadata and may attach ONE named reference image via reference:'<id>'; a missing file, unsupported type, image above 8 MiB or hash mismatch fails that read with a clear error. There is no automatic inference and no image bytes in the theme.

Complete executable definition. This is one spec value; unknown fields reject:
zpixel_theme({action:"add",theme:"soft-workshop",txid:"soft-workshop-add",spec:${JSON.stringify(THEME_EXAMPLE)}})

Read the production pack for a concrete task. It returns identity, brief, signature, every applicable or undecidable hard rule, tokens/bindings, capability notes and a review checklist; it never claims pass without a scene. Omitted context stays unknown. view:'full' also returns the definition. sections narrows optional detail, never hard rules. list returns summaries only:
zpixel_theme({action:"read",theme:"soft-workshop",context:{assetClass:"prop",nativeSize:[32,32],view:"top_down",channels:["beauty","normalmap"]},view:"working"})
zpixel_theme({action:"read",theme:"soft-workshop",revision:0,view:"full"})
zpixel_theme({action:"list",query:"workshop",limit:10})

Revise with the observed revision and a complete replacement. The old revision stays immutable; a pinned scene does not follow the head:
zpixel_theme({action:"revise",theme:"soft-workshop",expected_revision:0,txid:"soft-workshop-r1",spec:${JSON.stringify(THEME_EXAMPLE_REVISED)}})

Bind a pinned revision. Create resolves the theme before scene validation, merges namespaced resources and theme defaults (palette, ramps, materials, lights, ambient, exposure, camera, root outline, default material), and lets explicit initial scene fields win. Initial nodes may therefore reference namespaced palette/material IDs. It does not construct nodes, paint pixels or force geometry. Per-node bindings are explicit after the nodes exist. Resource ids are prefixed theme.<id>.<key>, so painted pixels reference the namespaced palette id:
zpixel_scene({action:"create",document:"crate",txid:"crate-create",theme:{id:"soft-workshop",revision:0,context:{assetClass:"prop",nativeSize:[32,32],view:"top_down",channels:["beauty"]}},scene:{width:32,height:32,nodes:[{id:"crate",name:"Crate",kind:"part",geometry:{type:"box",center:[0,1,0],size:[4,2,4]}},{id:"crate-label",name:"Crate label",kind:"layer",parent:"crate",appearanceOf:"crate",width:4,height:4,pixelSize:1,basis:{u:[1,0,0],v:[0,0,-1]},transform:{position:[-2,2,2]},castShadow:false,outline:{enabled:false},pixels:[null,"theme.soft-workshop.accent","theme.soft-workshop.accent",null,"theme.soft-workshop.accent",null,null,"theme.soft-workshop.accent","theme.soft-workshop.accent",null,null,"theme.soft-workshop.accent",null,"theme.soft-workshop.accent","theme.soft-workshop.accent",null]}]}})
zpixel_scene({action:"edit",document:"crate",revision:0,txid:"crate-theme-bind",preview:true,theme:{id:"soft-workshop",revision:0,bindings:[{node:"crate",material:"wood",outline:true},{node:"crate-label",appearanceShading:true}]}})

Create defaults applyDefaults:true; edit defaults false. Edit always merges namespaced resources and only the listed node bindings; unlisted nodes keep their values. Global lights/ambient/exposure/camera/root outline/defaultMaterial apply only when applyDefaults:true. Explicit initial palette/ramp/material entries that collide with theme resource IDs are preserved on create and listed in theme_changes.preservedExplicit; theme defaults do not overwrite them.

Theme resource application is additive: existing theme-namespaced palette/ramps/materials are never removed automatically, because old nodes may still reference them. On edit or replace, entries from the previous theme namespace that are absent from the new revision are retained and listed in theme_changes.retainedResources; older retained entries may also remain, and scene limits apply. theme_changes also reports defaultsApplied, resources ({palette,ramps,materials,lights} ID arrays), preservedExplicit and retainedResources ({palette,ramps,materials} ID arrays), removedLights (light IDs), globalFields and nodeBindings; theme:null yields metadataOnly/detached.

A material binding names a theme material key, not a scene id. outline:true applies the theme outline token and outline:false explicitly disables the target outline; appearanceShading:true applies the token and false leaves it unchanged. Colliding theme-managed resources in the same namespace error unless overwrite:true; a different current pin (hash/revision) errors unless replace:true. theme:null on edit detaches metadata only, leaving concrete values and already-applied resources. The scene stores the canonical snapshot, context and overrides, so rendering and export do not follow the library head and survive a missing theme store. A snapshot is a reproducibility record, not a review: it does not verify the theme source observations, and binding or reading is not a compliance pass.

Inspect the bound scene; inspect returns {document,revision,theme_report}, computed from the actual beauty buffer, not a diagnostic raster, and fixes scope from scene.theme.context plus actual native size. Statuses are pass, fail, needs_review, unsupported, not_applicable and waived. Mechanical checks cover final opaque color membership/count, binary alpha, padding, canvas, effective physical part outline configuration, camera, light count and appearance shading. Visual checks never become machine passes. must_not inverts a mechanical predicate; should is advisory and never blocks; unknown scope is needs_review with pending mechanical status, not pass. Overrides waive a rule and preserve deviations; they never rewrite the snapshot. blocked is true for a non-waived must/must_not mechanical failure or a required unsupported feature. There is no aesthetic score, percentage or part-count gate:
zpixel_inspect({document:"crate",kind:"theme"})

Preview and export share theme_report. Draft export preserves fail/pending/unsupported; reviewed beauty export blocks a blocked theme_report before writing bytes. Export embeds theme.json from the pinned snapshot, binding metadata and theme_report; a missing external store does not block export. Normalmap export uses the underlying beauty buffer for the same report; the normal map image is not checked against the beauty palette. Exports remain v0.5.3-dev-addressed; the renderer is still 0.3.6:
zpixel_render({document:"crate"})
zpixel_export({document:"crate",revision:1,draft:true})
zpixel_export({document:"crate",revision:1,draft:false,review:{identity:true,nativeReadable:true,lighting:true,note:"Crate reads at 32 px: ink boundary, ochre mass and teal label remain distinct."}})

A rebind is an explicit atomic edit: applyDefaults:true brings the new revision global defaults, replace:true accepts the different pin, and overwrite:true replaces same-namespace resources. Detach removes metadata only; concrete values and already-applied resources remain:
zpixel_scene({action:"edit",document:"crate",revision:1,txid:"crate-theme-r1",theme:{id:"soft-workshop",revision:1,applyDefaults:true,replace:true,overwrite:true,bindings:[{node:"crate",material:"wood",outline:true},{node:"crate-label",appearanceShading:true}]}})
zpixel_scene({action:"edit",document:"crate",revision:2,txid:"crate-theme-detach",theme:null})

Exact palette and lit. A palette check scans final opaque beauty RGBA including outline pixels. Lit parts multiply material brightness and interpolate ramps; lit appearance also scales sampled pixels. Final RGB can therefore leave the token set even when every input used a token. Declaring palette as must is honest only when the output path is unlit/flat or the actual ramp range is proven in-palette; otherwise the check may fail legitimately. unlit keeps sampled RGB exact but still uses geometry for depth, normal, shadow and outline. Do not silently switch shading to satisfy a palette rule; record the decision or an override. color_count has the same issue. The example keeps palette advisory (should) and unlit hard.

Unsupported and constraints. outline.width other than 1, material.specular above 0, colored lights, view-frame point lights, soft or dashed outlines, mirror reflection, CSG, animation and arbitrary scripts are not supported. Supported-shape unsupported values may be saved, but compatibility must report blockers with path/feature/reason/required and must not silently drop or coerce them; a required unsupported token blocks a conforming binding/export, while drafts remain allowed. Unknown token fields reject. Tokens omitted are unspecified; lights:[] is an explicit no-lights choice. light frame is world or view; view directions compile through the actual scene camera basis, with the canonical top_down camera used and disclosed only when no actual camera is supplied. No theme can add geometry, paint pixels, force a part count, replace modeling or turn a flat layer into a solid. Theme store touches only .zpixel/themes, never assets or documents. Theme resource application is additive: old theme-namespaced palette/ramps/materials are retained, not silently pruned, and scene limits apply. Bounded data: 256 KiB per revision, 64 rules, token ids at most 48 chars so namespaced ids stay within 128. Rebind an existing work only as an explicit atomic edit with preview; do not auto-repaint it.`;
const tools=`Seven tools: scene, render, inspect, normalmap, export, guide, theme. scene actions create/open/list/edit/undo/redo/migrate; theme actions add/read/list/revise. add/revise save a complete spec and return a fixed revision/hash; read returns a working pack and never claims pass; list returns summaries. Bind a pinned revision with the outer theme parameter of scene; theme:null on edit detaches metadata only. Scene theme application reports theme_changes with resources, preservedExplicit, retainedResources, removedLights, globalFields and nodeBindings. Resource application is additive: existing theme-namespaced palette/ramps/materials are never removed automatically, and old entries are retained and reported. Theme checks reuse inspect kind:'theme', preview and export. list pages an alphabetical catalog of existing user documents: offset integer >=0 (default0), limit integer 1..50 (default50), and returns nextOffset. It is not a set of examples and grants no permission to reuse other projects. Scene edits are atomic and revision/txid protected. Common edit shapes: transform {id,translate?/rotate?/scale?,space?}; node_set {id,values:{...}}; paint {id,ops:[...]}; palette_set {id,color:"#RRGGBB"}. node_set replaces each supplied field wholesale (no deep merge) and values.pixels replaces a whole layer; paint edits regions. node_set values.appearanceOf:null unbinds a bound appearance layer and removes appearanceShading; see painting for binding and shading. render accepts transient camera/fit/isolate/physicalOnly/expectedRevision/expectedHash; an observation returns delivery:false plus an observation receipt and never writes, and a supplied camera is always an observation even when it matches the delivery view. inspect accepts the same fields; kind:'view' returns an image preview plus receipt, pixel/region/spatial use the same camera and physicalOnly, and expected pins apply to every kind. Scene responses include authoring:{contract,structureHash,contentHash,observation,deliveryPreview}. On a new spatial document, scene preview:true is the automatic fitted non-delivery physicalOnly working view with preview.workingView:true, not the final delivery preview; a working-view failure returns previewError and does not fall back to delivery. Explicit render without overrides is final delivery beauty. inspect separates authored relation intent, world geometry observations, optional camera projection and theme_report. normalmap is sprite-local 2D data for engine lighting; render mode:'normals' is still the world-space diagnostic, and export is still beauty. Older renderer versions never overwrite old versions. Draft export is available without image input; reviewed export requires current preview and honest review, blocks a blocked theme_report, and for a new spatial document requires a delivered whole-scene nonparallel observation at the current structureHash, at least 20 degrees from the delivery viewing axis (parallel and antiparallel do not count; raw deliveryAngleDegrees 20..160). The gate status is observed, not-observed, flat-only-exempt, legacy-none or waived; structureReview:{waived:true,reason} is a recorded 3-360 character waiver, never a pass. normalmap reports observation.countsAsObservation:false and cannot satisfy the gate.`;
const limits=`Canvas/layers256x256, nodes256, depth32, white lights8, colors256, batch512, estimated render work32M, stored history12/receipts128/32MiB. Spatial query results bounded. Anchor placement rejects underconstrained roll. Box SAT exact; sampled complex geometry may remain unresolved. No holes, CSG, physics solver, colored lighting, mirror reflection or animation. Normal maps do not recover 3D from painted pixels, supply mesh tangents, validate engine import or unlight a beauty sprite. Bound appearance adds no physical geometry; see nodes/painting for binding, object-space sampling, shading and normal-map limits. Old documents without appearanceOf keep old pixels. Themes: one per scene, no subtree or multi-theme engine; 256KiB/revision and 64 rules; unsupported tokens may be saved but block a conforming export; resource application is additive, so replacing a theme retains old theme-namespaced palette/ramps/materials (retainedResources) and scene limits apply; no image analysis, geometry/pixel generation, part-count gate or aesthetic score. Observation is orthographic only: no perspective, orbit editor, fov or turntable; fit changes framing, not direction. An observation receipt records what was observed, not geometric correctness or approval. Isolated and zoom-only observations do not satisfy the reviewed-export gate.`;
export const GUIDES={overview,camera,observation,nodes,geometry,anchors,spatial,painting,lighting,normalmap,migration,theme,tools,limits};
export function guide(topic='overview'){if(!Object.hasOwn(GUIDES,topic))throw new Error('Unknown guide topic. Choose '+Object.keys(GUIDES).join(', '));return{topic,text:GUIDES[topic],topics:Object.keys(GUIDES)};}
